{
  "image_name": "ad9f959bb38593fbfd92b81e48a4dd64.png",
  "prompt_text": "This figure is a block diagram of a three-electrode electrochemical measurement circuit, essentially a potentiostat with readout options. At the top, the electrochemical cell is shown with counter (CE), working (WE), and reference (RE) electrodes. The main idea is to control the voltage of the working electrode relative to the reference electrode while measuring the resulting current from oxidation or reduction reactions.\n\nA control amplifier drives the counter electrode with a correction signal so the desired applied voltage is maintained at the cell. An electrometer monitors the working-electrode potential referenced to the reference electrode, indicating that the voltage is sensed with very high input impedance. In parallel, the current at the working electrode is routed through a ranging resistor and an I–V converter, which turns the electrochemical current into a measurable voltage.\n\nThe lower part shows signal selection and digitization: a MUX appears to choose between the measured electrode voltage and another working-electrode path (labeled WE2), then sends the selected analog signal to an ADC. Overall, the diagram emphasizes the feedback control loop and the conversion of tiny electrochemical currents into voltages suitable for electronic measurement.",
